# DeployV050 settings for script/deploy-v050.sh <network> [--broadcast]
#
# Copy deploy/v050/<network>.env.example (or this file) to deploy/v050/<network>.env and fill it in.
# deploy/v050/*.env is git-ignored: never commit a filled-in file. Placeholders only in this template.
# The wrapper sources exactly one of these files with bash, so keep values quoted when they contain spaces
# or `<`/`>`. Placeholders ("<...>") are rejected by the wrapper.
# forge auto-loads the repo root .env. The wrapper exports every variable below (empty = default) and REFUSES to run
# while the root .env defines any of them (or ETH_* / FOUNDRY_*), so a root .env cannot change this deployment.
# An empty value (`NAME=` or `NAME=""`) always means "use the default".

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Network
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Informational name, must match the <network> argument (polygon | amoy).
CHAIN_NAME="<polygon-or-amoy>"
# RPC of that network. The wrapper checks `cast chain-id` against the network (polygon=137, amoy=80002).
RPC_URL="<https://your-rpc-url>"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Required (the script fails fast naming the missing variable)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Backend voucher signer (EIP-712 StakeVoucher signatures must come from this address).
VOUCHER_SIGNER="<0x-voucher-signer-address>"
# Receiver of seized principal.
TREASURY="<0x-treasury-address>"
# ALL THREE ceilings (MAX_EXTRA_APY_BPS, MAX_EXTRA_LIMIT_TOTAL, MAX_EXTRA_LIMIT_PER_CELL) must be AT OR ABOVE the
# largest value in the backend's VIP staking perk grid (backend admin: VIP perk config page). The backend refuses
# to sign a voucher whose extras exceed a ceiling, and staking is voucher-only, so every VIP level above a ceiling
# could not stake AT ALL. Raise the ceiling or lower the grid first; setMaxExtra*() can change a ceiling later.
# Highest extra APY a voucher may carry, in bps (500 = 5.00%). Liveops decides the value.
MAX_EXTRA_APY_BPS="<bps>"
# Ceiling on a voucher's total bonus budget, in token wei. That budget is GLOBAL: one per wallet across every
# phase, and advancing the phase does not refill it. Liveops decides the value.
MAX_EXTRA_LIMIT_TOTAL="<token-wei>"
# Highest share of that budget a voucher may allow in one (phase, period) cell, in token wei. Must be non-zero
# whenever MAX_EXTRA_LIMIT_TOTAL is, or no voucher can ever spend its bonus. Liveops decides the value.
MAX_EXTRA_LIMIT_PER_CELL="<token-wei>"
# Furthest ahead of now a voucher's validUntil may sit, in seconds. Must be non-zero. MUST be >= the
# backend's configured validity_seconds (backend bounds it 60-1800) or every voucher reverts. Ops sets it.
MAX_VOUCHER_VALIDITY="<seconds>"
# Optional but strongly recommended: the backend's staking voucher validity_seconds (StakingVoucherConfig, 60-1800)
# - the on-chain ceiling MAX_VOUCHER_VALIDITY must be >= this. When set, deploy-v050.sh refuses to deploy and
# verify-v050.sh refuses to pass a ceiling below it. When unset, both can only check against the backend's hard
# maximum of 1800 and warn instead. The contract never reads this; it is a cross-repo consistency check.
# BACKEND_VALIDITY_SECONDS="<seconds>"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deployer / signing (used by the wrapper, never by the Solidity script)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Address that sends the transactions; also used as --sender when simulating.
DEPLOYER_ADDRESS="<0x-deployer-address>"
# Preferred: a Foundry keystore account name (`cast wallet import <name> --interactive`).
DEPLOYER_ACCOUNT="<keystore-account-name>"
# Discouraged fallback, only if no keystore can be used. Leave commented out. Never commit it.
# Handed to the script as an environment variable (vm.startBroadcast(key)), never on forge's command line.
# PRIVATE_KEY=
# Etherscan v2 API key (POLYGONSCAN_API_KEY is accepted too). Needed TWICE:
#   - deploy-v050.sh adds --verify to the broadcast only when this is set. Leave it commented out and the
#     deployment lands unverified, which is exactly what happened to the first Amoy v0.4.0 deployment;
#   - verify-v050.sh needs it to verify a deployment after the fact.
# Set it before broadcasting and you never need the second step. Passed to forge as ETHERSCAN_API_KEY in the
# environment, never as --etherscan-api-key.
# ETHERSCAN_API_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional (leave commented out to use the default)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Source v0.2.4 contract to copy. Default: the per-chain table in script/DeployV050.s.sol.
# SOURCE_STAKING="<0x-source-staking>"
# Worth-aggregating RequirementCheckerV2 the backend reads (only used to print the manual list step).
# Default: the per-chain table in script/DeployV050.s.sol.
# REQUIREMENT_CHECKER_V2="<0x-requirement-checker-v2>"
# BOTH contracts are two-step (Ownable2Step): the deploy only NOMINATES NEW_OWNER and the deployer stays owner
# of both until NEW_OWNER calls acceptOwnership() on the staking contract AND on the LimitController. The script
# prints this as a manual step; the deployment is not finished until both calls have landed.
# NEW_OWNER="<0x-new-owner>"
# Default false: an address holding two roles (VOUCHER_SIGNER that is also the deployer, NEW_OWNER, TREASURY or
# an admin; TREASURY that is the staking token, a staking contract, NEW_OWNER, or an address in ADMINS) FAILS
# the preflight before anything is deployed. true downgrades every such clash to a printed warning. Testnets
# only: on mainnet a leaked backend signing key that is also the owner turns a signing incident into an
# ownership one, and an admin that is also the TREASURY can both seize deposits and receive them.
# Duplicate entries in ADMINS are always a warning only, never fatal.
# ALLOW_SHARED_ROLES=false
# Comma-separated, added with addContractAdmin.
# ADMINS="<0x-admin-1>,<0x-admin-2>"
# Default false: staking on the new contract stays closed until opened deliberately.
# OPEN_STAKING=false
# Default 0 = no funding; >0 = approve + provideReward from the deployer.
# REWARD_TOP_UP=0
# CSV `wallet,phase,period,limit` (limit in token wei), checked row by row against the source controller;
# limit-0 rows are dropped (0 = "use the default" on v0.2.4, "no base allowance" on the new one -- not a block;
# a voucher bonus still stakes on top, and blocking outright is walletBlocked).
# Must live under deploy/v050/ (the only deploy folder foundry.toml lets the script read).
# WALLET_LIMITS_FILE="deploy/v050/<network>-wallet-limits.csv"
# Extra forge arguments, word-split. Quote the value. A flag named here is NOT added again by the wrapper,
# so this is also how you override one of the robustness defaults below with a different spelling.
# ALLOWLIST: only these may appear, and anything else is rejected BY NAME before the build runs.
#   boolean:    -v -vv -vvv -vvvv -vvvvv --gas-report --json --silent --slow --isolate --legacy
#               --force --no-cache --sizes
#   with value: --gas-estimate-multiplier --gas-price --priority-gas-price --gas-limit --rpc-timeout
#               --timeout --retries --delay --out --cache-path --optimizer-runs --evm-version
#               (written as "--flag value" or "--flag=value")
# A bare word, anything after "--", and every other flag are refused. In particular --resume is refused: it
# re-submits a cached broadcast, so it SENDS transactions while the wrapper's banner still says "simulation".
# Choose the signer with DEPLOYER_ACCOUNT/PRIVATE_KEY, the network with RPC_URL and verification with
# ETHERSCAN_API_KEY above - never through FORGE_ARGS.
# FORGE_ARGS="--out /tmp/out --cache-path /tmp/cache"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Broadcast robustness (wrapper-only; see README "Why the first Amoy broadcast ran out of gas")
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The wrapper always passes these to forge, and prints them in its banner:
#   --isolate  every top-level call is simulated in its own EVM context, so warm storage cannot carry between
#              calls that become separate transactions on chain. Measured on forks of both networks this did
#              not change the estimates; it is kept as defence in depth, not as the fix.
#   --slow     one transaction at a time, each confirmed before the next is estimated and sent, so estimates
#              are made against the state the previous transactions left behind.
#
# Headroom on top of the estimate, in percent (forge's own default is 130). 200 doubles the estimate: it
# absorbs the cost that addStakingPeriod (which re-sorts a growing list) and pushStakingPhase (2 storage
# slots per period) gain as earlier transactions land, and costs nothing when unused - unspent gas is refunded,
# only the gas actually burnt is paid for. Raise it (250-300) on a chain where you would rather not retry.
# GAS_ESTIMATE_MULTIPLIER=200
# Seconds forge waits for a single RPC request. Public endpoints stall; the default of 45 is optimistic.
# RPC_TIMEOUT=120
# Seconds forge waits for a sent transaction's receipt before giving up on it (--broadcast only).
# TX_TIMEOUT=600
# Contract VERIFICATION retries and the delay between them (only used when a verification key is set).
# NOTE: in forge 1.7.1 --retries/--delay apply to verification only; they do NOT retry failed RPC calls.
# VERIFY_RETRIES=10
# VERIFY_DELAY=15
# Retries for the wrappers' OWN chain reads (cast chain-id / cast code / cast call), which forge's flags do not
# cover. Both scripts retry a failed read this many times, and when they finally give up they print cast's real
# error with any URL redacted, so a flaky free-tier endpoint is never reported as a wrong address.
# RPC_RETRIES=3
# RPC_RETRY_DELAY=3

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Resuming a broadcast that died partway
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Set these to the addresses the failed run printed (or the ones in
# broadcast/DeployV050.s.sol/<chainid>/run-latest.json) and run the same command again. The script then reuses
# those contracts, checks every setting/period/phase/limit and sends only the transactions that are still
# missing, ending in the same full self-check. Both contracts must still be owned by the deployer.
# Leave RESUME_CONTROLLER empty if the LimitController was never deployed; RESUME_STAKING is then required.
# RESUME_STAKING="<0x-partially-configured-staking>"
# RESUME_CONTROLLER="<0x-partially-configured-limit-controller>"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Verifying a deployment after the fact
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Two different things use the pair below.
#
# 1. script/verify-v050.sh <network> - Etherscan source verification.
#    Only needed when the addresses cannot be read from broadcast/DeployV050.s.sol/<chainid>/run-latest.json
#    (a different machine, or a run whose broadcast log is gone). When both are unset the script reads that file
#    and says so. Constructor arguments are never taken from here: they are read from the contracts themselves.
#
# 2. script/deploy-v050.sh <network> --verify-only - the CONFIGURATION self-check, re-run against the chain.
#    This is the mandatory step after a broadcast: the self-check inside a broadcast run reads the simulation's
#    state, not what the network actually mined. --verify-only sends nothing (no --broadcast) and re-reads every
#    copied value from the two contracts below, so it also confirms the ownership hand-off - it accepts BOTH the
#    deployer still owning with NEW_OWNER pending AND NEW_OWNER having accepted on both contracts, but the two
#    contracts must be at the SAME stage: accepting one and forgetting the other fails loudly
#    ("check: half-finished hand-off"), naming the contract that is still pending.
#    It requires VERIFY_STAKING, VERIFY_CONTROLLER and DEPLOYER_ADDRESS, and the rest of this file must still
#    hold the values the deployment was broadcast with, or the check compares against the wrong settings.
#    Two deliberate exceptions to "must still hold the deployment's values":
#      - OPEN_STAKING and the other action-availability flags are admin-mutable and the hand-off ends by
#        opening staking, so a mismatch there is LOGGED, not fatal;
#      - REWARD_TOP_UP is checked as rewardPool() >= REWARD_TOP_UP (not merely non-zero), so once rewards have
#        been paid out and the pool has legitimately dropped, set REWARD_TOP_UP=0 for verification runs.
# VERIFY_STAKING="<0x-deployed-staking>"
# VERIFY_CONTROLLER="<0x-deployed-limit-controller>"
# VERIFY_RETRIES / VERIFY_DELAY above are shared with the deploy wrapper.
#
# VERIFY_ONLY is the env variable script/DeployV050.s.sol itself reads to select that mode. Do NOT set it here:
# deploy-v050.sh sets it from the --verify-only argument and clears it otherwise, so the mode is always chosen on
# the command line and a stray VERIFY_ONLY=true can never turn a deployment into a silent no-op.
# VERIFY_ONLY=
